# Force LF on checkout regardless of a contributor's core.autocrlf, so a Windows
# clone (default core.autocrlf=true) cannot silently turn a committed LF text file
# into CRLF. Three CI failures already traced to exactly one instance of this:
# a Windows checkout converted plugin/requirements.lock to CRLF, and
# scripts/plugin_meta.py's runtime_key() hashes LOCK.read_bytes() -- raw bytes, no
# newline normalization -- so the hash stopped matching the committed
# plugin/scripts/runtime.env and CI reported the runtime files as stale.
#
# This is repo-wide rather than a one-line carve-out for that one file, because
# the lock is not the only file in this position:
#   - plugin/scripts/runtime.env and plugin/scripts/uv-checksums are generated
#     from the same lock (scripts/plugin_meta.py) and are line-parsed, not just
#     hashed: `. sourced` by the POSIX plugin/scripts/start and
#     plugin/scripts/ensure_uv (an embedded \r lands inside the shell variable's
#     value, not just the line ending), and `for /f`-parsed by their .cmd twins.
#   - plugin/scripts/start, plugin/scripts/ensure_uv and plugin/scripts/capture_hook
#     are POSIX shell scripts invoked by their shebang; a CRLF-corrupted shebang
#     line breaks exec on Linux/macOS outright ("bad interpreter").
#   - plugin/scripts/*.cmd are committed LF on purpose even though CRLF is the
#     native Windows convention: a past bug ("the for /f quoting trap that made
#     ensure_uv.cmd report every checksum as a mismatch") came from a stray CR
#     landing in one of these files, and cmd.exe's `for /f` parses LF-terminated
#     files fine. Left to core.autocrlf with no override, a Windows checkout would
#     convert these to CRLF and reopen that exact class of bug.
#
# Verified before adding this: no file in the repository is currently committed
# with CRLF, and the sole binary asset (assets/memorysafe-icon.png) is already
# correctly classified as binary by git's own content-based detection under
# text=auto -- so this rule changes nothing about what is checked in today, only
# what a future Windows checkout is allowed to do to it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The one carve-out, and it is cmd.exe's fault rather than a preference.
#
# The note above says *.cmd is committed LF on purpose because `for /f` parses
# LF-terminated files fine. That part is still true, and every `for /f` in these
# scripts reads a *data* file (runtime.env, uv-checksums) or a command's output,
# none of which this rule touches: those stay LF, so the stray-CR bug it guards
# against cannot come back through them.
#
# What `for /f` being fine does not cover is `call :label`. cmd.exe resolves a label
# by byte offset, and on an LF-only batch file it lands mid-line and reports "The
# system cannot find the batch label specified". Whether it does depends on where the
# label happens to sit, so start.cmd worked by luck: inserting ONE `rem` line
# anywhere above :write_cli_shim makes the call fail, the CLI shim is never written,
# and nothing says so -- the server still starts, so only the first-run E2E notices.
# Measured on Windows 11 with start.cmd at 178 lines; 0 filler lines pass, 1, 5 and
# 24 all fail, and the same file with CRLF passes at every size.
#
# eol=crlf changes the checkout, not the blob: git still stores these LF, so nothing
# about what is committed changes, and diffs stay clean. It applies on Linux and
# macOS too, where these files are never executed -- the tests that read them use
# Path.read_text, which normalizes newlines.
plugin/scripts/*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Explicit rather than relying solely on text=auto's own binary detection for the
# one binary file in the tree today, and self-documenting for the next one added.
*.png binary
